@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@
+VERDICT: Pilot `codex exec` on isolated report work first; keep bundle editing and Docker gates on the present opencode path until equivalent permissions and output quality are demonstrated.
+
+## 1. CLI capabilities and auto-mode fit
+
+**Finding.** `CLI_MATRIX.csv` compares the three worker paths. All can start non-interactively, resume a saved session, and emit machine-readable output. Their controls differ: Codex has an explicit read-only/workspace-write sandbox and a last-message file; Claude has print-mode JSON/stream-JSON, named sessions, tool/permission modes; opencode's present `auto.py` already creates SHA-titled HTTP sessions, sends prompts, and watches status. `codex exec --help`, `codex exec resume --help`, `claude --help`, `opencode run --help`; `auto.py:293-379,613-645,724-743,1150-1202`. The master's successful Codex smoke test was **12 seconds, 7,146 reported tokens** with stdin closed (`REQUEST.md:F`); it was one file-count task, not a quality benchmark.
+
+**Evidence.** `auto.py:105-142` gives opencode workers project edit access and Docker-capable bash while denying paid harbor queueing; oracle/nop exceptions are explicit. `auto.py:68,1987` polls every 60 seconds. `~/.codex/config.toml:2-3` has model and reasoning-effort keys (values intentionally omitted). `find ~/.codex/sessions -type f` found 25 files; a read-only JSON key scan found 1,495 `token_count` and 1,492 `token_usage_record` events. `find ~/.claude/projects -maxdepth 2 -name '*.jsonl'` found 22 files; JSON key scan found usage fields in 11,298 assistant records. Claude's `stats-cache.json` reports lastComputedDate 2026-10-24, 19 sessions, 29,958 messages, 12,809,383 output and 2,534,723,674 cache-read tokens for claude-opus-5-5; it does **not** establish current remaining quota. `REQUEST.md:E` says Claude login rotates among friends.
+
+**Recommendation R1.** Add a worker adapter beside `auto.py` that records `{sha, provider, session_id, pid, output_path, started, ended, exit_code}` in the run state; parse each CLI's JSON events, validate the existing step `result_<sha>.json`, and keep board and retry semantics intact. Use `stdin=DEVNULL`, a bounded runtime, stdout/stderr files, and explicit failure classification. Expected gain: automated Codex/Claude launches with the existing board workflow; no measured speed saving yet. Risk: CLI process exit may not mean a valid task result. Verify with a no-cost, local fake JSONL process, then a single authorized live pilot. `auto.py:1150-1202` is the current spawn/send seam.
+
+**Recommendation R2.** Default Codex report jobs to `-s read-only -a never -C <input-root> --json -o <output-file> --skip-git-repo-check` with an allowed output directory provided by the host; persist sessions for resume. Codex's `-o` needs a writable output location, so test actual confinement before production. Expected gain: less manual collection and fewer accidental writes; not a measured token saving. Risk: read-only sandbox may block the output file or local commands. Verify by diffing the input tree before/after and checking output, exit code, and JSON schema. **UNVERIFIED:** Docker socket access and exact path confinement under Codex workspace-write were not tested.
+
+**Recommendation R3.** Do not route heavy work to `claude -p` until Ayush sets a per-run quota policy for the rotating account. When allowed, start with `--output-format json`, explicit allowed tools and permission mode, and a named session. Expected gain: a second worker option; speed/quality gain **UNVERIFIED**. Risk: account limits, silent invalid-settings handling, or a permission prompt in print mode. Verify account and quota immediately before launch, then compare its JSON cost/usage and result file against the baseline. `claude --help` describes these flags and its print-mode trust/settings behavior; `REQUEST.md:E` records the rotation.
+
+## 2. Measured cost and time by step
+
+**Finding.** `scripts/measure_workers.py` joined `master_chat_tasks/board.json` rows with status `done` and step prefix P2/P9/P11/P12/P13 to top-level opencode sessions whose title equals the board SHA, using the live database **read-only**. `worker_measurements.csv` has the 190 matched rows; measurement window is issued 2026-10-22 through 2026-10-30. Counts: P11 74 of 76 completed board rows, P12 48 of 53, P13 42 of 43, P9 17 of 18, P2 9 of 9. Missing matches were excluded. The table gives medians of recorded session values. Span is `session.time_updated - time_created`, **including** pauses, retries and follow-up turns; it is not active model time. Costs are opencode's recorded `session.cost`, not a subscription charge. Reasoning is shown separately and must not automatically be added to output tokens. Source: DB `session` columns (schema in script query), `scripts/measure_workers.py`, `worker_measurements.csv`.
+
+| Step | Matched n | Median span min | Output tokens | Reasoning tokens | Cache-read tokens | Recorded cost USD |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| P11 QC | 74 | 27.4 | 85,628 | 33,904 | 11,024,052 | 8.97 |
+| P12 fix | 48 | 28.5 | 70,027 | 20,970 | 10,128,890 | 6.23 |
+| P13 blind | 42 | 5.1 | 18,665 | 8,212 | 1,271,586 | 1.25 |
+| P9 harden | 17 | 55.9 | 123,153 | 59,686 | 18,654,958 | 12.39 |
+| P2 build | 9 | 151.6 | 217,985 | 153,400 | 27,445,646 | 24.21 |
+
+**Finding.** The prior speed study measured active-versus-wait components more precisely than session span: for 164 SHA-titled sessions from 26–30 Oct, 245.1 aggregate session-hours comprised 85.7 h human/master wait (35%), 55.5 h sleep/poll (23%), 14.8 h other tools (6%), and 89.1 h residual (36%). It counted 382 `sleep ...; docker ps` loops in that window and sampled zero running containers in 43% of those Docker polls. This is an aggregate of overlapping sessions, **not** calendar elapsed time or a guaranteed saving. `master_chat_work/20261030_speed_measure/MEASURE.md:77-120` and its definitions at `:79-83`.
+
+**Finding.** Token sources are partly observable. Completed issued prompt files matched to the board have median byte sizes P11 15,880 (68 files), P12 12,634 (48), P13 4,996 (41), P9 11,506 (16), P2 22,673 (9), computed from board prompt paths with the one-line Python command in this session's tool trace. Current common rules are 18,973 bytes (`wc -c prompts/templates/_COMMON_RULES_v5.md`). A stratified sample of 15 worker sessions in `scripts/inspect_tool_load.py` counted 21–563 tool parts per session and 0.15–4.40 MB serialized tool JSON per session; its command-mention counts show repeated `cat`, `sed`, `rg`, Docker and sleep calls. These byte counts are **not** token counts and the sample cannot allocate exact token share to rule rereads versus tool outputs. No per-step compaction count was recoverable from `session.time_compacting`: 0 of 535 session rows had it populated (`sqlite3 ... select count(*),sum(time_compacting is not null) from session`). **UNVERIFIED:** exact compaction contribution and billing impact of cached input.
